Title: THE VELVETEEN RABBIT
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from "The Velveteen Rabbit" by Margery Williams
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A modest nursery glows with morning light. A stocking bulges from the mantel, the VELVETEEN RABBIT wedged at its top -- fat, bunchy, brown-and-white spotted, with thread whiskers and pink-sateen ears, a sprig of holly between his paws.

(MUSIC: gentle, twinkling holiday theme)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid. For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

The Boy laughs, then AUNTS and UNCLES arrive. Tissue paper rustles. New parcels are torn open. The Rabbit is set aside on the nursery floor.

(SOUND: excited voices and paper rustling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And in the excitement of looking at all the new presents the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

The mechanical toys and MODEL BOAT look down on him. TIMOTHY the wooden lion puts on airs. Only the old SKIN HORSE is kind.

(SOUND: tinny clockwork whirrs, fading to quiet)

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Sunlight stripes the nursery floor. The VELVETEEN RABBIT and SKIN HORSE lie side by side near the fender. The Skin Horse is bald in patches, seams showing, tail hairs long gone.

(MUSIC: soft, thoughtful strings)

RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago. But once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

(SOUND: distant nursery clock ticking steadily)

The Rabbit sighs, a small sad sound.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He longed to become Real, to know what it felt like. Yet the idea of growing shabby was rather sad.

INT. NURSERY - EVENING

NANA bustles through the toy-strewn nursery, a small whirlwind. The VELVETEEN RABBIT tumbles softly wherever she throws him.

(MUSIC: playful but hurried piano)

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drags the Rabbit out by one ear and drops him into THE BOY's arms.

(SOUND: fabric rustle and footsteps retreating)

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Moonlight falls across the small bed. The Boy hugs the VELVETEEN RABBIT tight under the covers, making tunnels in the blankets.

(MUSIC: tender, lullaby-like melody)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed. The Boy used to talk to him and made nice tunnels for him under the bedclothes that he said were like the burrows the real rabbits lived in.

The Rabbit snuggles under the Boy's warm chin as the child drifts to sleep.

(SOUND: soft breathing and distant night-light hiss)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy. He never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

EXT. GARDEN - SPRING DAY

Bright spring light fills the garden. The Boy pushes the VELVETEEN RABBIT in a wheelbarrow, then builds a fairy hut under the raspberry canes. Later, the Boy is called away; the Rabbit is left alone on the lawn until dusk.

(MUSIC: fresh, blooming outdoor theme)

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

The Boy sits up in bed, stretching out his hands.

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Rabbit's boot-button eyes catch a new light of wisdom.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last. The nursery magic had happened to him, and he was Real.

(SOUND: birdsong swelling, then soft wind through leaves)

EXT. WOOD - SUMMER EVENING

Golden evening light filters through the trees. The VELVETEEN RABBIT sits in a nest of bracken. Two lively WILD RABBITS creep from the tall ferns, twitching noses, hopping easily on strong hind legs.

(MUSIC: curious, playful woodland motif)

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
It's as easy as anything.

The Wild Rabbit hops sideways and stands on his hind legs, then whirls in a dance.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

RABBIT
I am Real! The Boy said so!

The Wild Rabbits stamp and vanish in a flash of white tails.

(SOUND: sudden rustle of bracken, then silence broken only by ants)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Velveteen Rabbit was all alone. For a long time he lay very still, hoping they would come back. But they never returned.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

The nursery is dimmed by illness. The Boy lies flushed and hot, talking in his sleep. The VELVETEEN RABBIT stays hidden under the bedclothes, never stirring.

(MUSIC: low, anxious undertone)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy was ill. Strange people came and went, and a light burned all night. The little Rabbit lay there, afraid that if they found him someone might take him away.

The fever breaks. The Boy sits up with picture-books. The Rabbit cuddles close.

(SOUND: fevered breathing slowing to calm)

EXT. GARDEN BALCONY - MORNING

Sun streams across the balcony. The Boy, wrapped in a shawl, listens as the DOCTOR and NANA speak nearby. The VELVETEEN RABBIT lies tangled in the discarded bedclothes, head peeping out.

(MUSIC: bright morning turning somber)

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more.

NANA bundles the Rabbit into a sack with old books and rubbish.

(SOUND: sackcloth scrape and distant birds)

EXT. BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

Behind the fowl-house, the sack sits untied in the shadows near a thicket of raspberry canes. The VELVETEEN RABBIT wriggles his head out, shivering, threadbare coat offering no warmth. He gazes at the remembered garden.

(MUSIC: lonely, aching strings)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night the Boy slept in a different bedroom with a new bunny. The little Rabbit lay among the old picture-books and felt very lonely. He thought of the Skin Horse and all that he had told him. Of what use was it to be loved and become Real if it all ended like this?

A single tear trickles down the Rabbit's shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

(SOUND: soft plink of tear hitting earth)

Where the tear fell, a mysterious flower pushes up -- emerald leaves, golden-cup blossom. The blossom opens. A beautiful FAIRY steps out, dressed in pearl and dewdrops.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

RABBIT
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to everyone.

She gathers him close, kisses his damp nose, and flies with him toward the wood.

(SOUND: magical chime as flower opens, then soft wing-like flutter)

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

Moonlight silvers the bracken in an open glade. Wild rabbits dance on the velvet grass. The FAIRY sets the VELVETEEN RABBIT down.

(MUSIC: wondrous, transformative swell)

FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever! Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit hesitates, ashamed of his old form. Then something tickles his nose. He lifts a hind leg to scratch -- and discovers real, strong hind legs. His velveteen is now soft brown fur. His ears twitch. He leaps, springing and whirling with joy.

(SOUND: joyful rabbit thumps on turf, music lifting to triumphant)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - SPRING DAY

Warm spring sunlight dapples the wood behind the house. THE BOY plays among the trees. Two rabbits creep from the bracken and peep at him. One is brown all over; the other has faint strange markings under his fur as though he had once been spotted. Something familiar lingers about his soft nose and round black eyes.

(MUSIC: gentle, nostalgic close)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy thought to himself: "Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!" But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

The Real Rabbit twitches his whiskers once, then bounds away into the bracken.

(SOUND: soft wind through leaves, fading to quiet)

FADE OUT.

THE END
